{"kind":"request","text":"Design note: one source for the guard grammar. Write notes/\u003cdate\u003e-guard-grammar-authority.md.\n\nBackground. Josh Rosen's article \"Architecting the 'No Skills' Application\" (X article 2112313639742935040, 2026-10-20) says to establish one authoritative definition of each contract and derive the rest, and that generated files should look generated, be hard to modify by accident, and fail verification when they no longer match their source. Hugh agreed on 2026-10-20 that this is the fourth gap in dap, below the application layout note and the history note. The planner filed this from that conversation.\n\nWhat the tree shows today (main 248aab8f, 2026-10-20). packages/derive/src/validate (22 files, 4,182 lines) is the static validator of a definition. The runtime judge in packages/derive/src (about 9.5k lines, consolidation survey of 2026-10-19) evaluates the same grammar at act time. The guard grammar and the forms of fields, handlers, sends, timed rules, holds and capacities are stated in both. The survey called a consolidation medium-term and risky. The contract package holds the types that both sides share. validatePackage (builder-room note, slice P1) adds a third reader of the same grammar at the package level. dap already has the drift pattern in two places: docs/mcp-tools.json is derived from the live tool list and a test refuses it when it differs (packages/mcp/test/inventory.test.ts, regenerated with MCP_WRITE_DOCS=1), and docs/lanes-reference.md is written by reference.mjs.\n\nThe note decides.\n1. The inventory: each rule of the grammar and where it is stated today (the validator, the runtime judge, validatePackage, docs/protocol.md, the contract types).\n2. The direction of authority, per group of rules. The choices: the runtime judge is the source and the validator is derived; or a grammar table in contract from which the validator's shape checks and the runtime's dispatch both derive; or the validator is the source and the runtime's checks are shown equal by a witness. The recommendation weighs replay (the runtime's judgment is what replay reproduces) and the requirement that the validator stay a pure bounded function.\n3. The drift witness: one corpus of definitions and refusals run through both sides, with equal refusal codes. The evidence rules' paired witnesses already have the shape: one input that passes and one that fails for that reason only.\n4. What \"looks generated\" means here, if anything is generated: a header, a location, and a check that refuses a hand edit.\n5. Slices. The note commissions no rewrite. The first slice is the witness.\n\nAcceptance. The inventory table; the decision per group; the witness design; the slices. Document only. Checker review before landing. The note names no product other than GitHub and cites the article by author and title. Implementation requests follow after it lands.\n\nPriority. Last of the three notes filed on 2026-10-20.","body":{"conditions":"Document only; checker review before landing; no product names other than GitHub in the note; implementation requests follow after it lands.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}